+## Subtopics
+
+### VolSDF
+A method that interprets MLP outputs as a signed distance field (SDF) and regularizes the gradient norm to 1 using Eikonal loss [S1]. It serves as the foundation for the SDF representation in HybridNeRF.
+
+### Nerfacto
+A method that uses a proposal network to improve sampling efficiency in neural radiance fields [S1]. HybridNeRF uses a similar proposal network.
+
+### Multi-resolution Triplanes
+A technique for featurizing 3D locations using 2D planes [S1]. HybridNeRF uses this for feature storage.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is significantly faster than standard NeRF because sphere tracing allows the use of far fewer samples per ray for the $>95\%$ of the scene that behaves as a surface [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The surfaceness grid requires memory for a $512^3$ grid of floats. The neural networks (SDF, Color, Feature Grids) also consume memory, but the reduction in sampling count reduces the computational load per pixel [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves real-time framerates of at least 36 FPS for virtual-reality resolutions (2K×2K) [S1].
-*   **Accuracy:** Improves error rates by 15–30% compared to state-of-the-art baselines, including rasterization-based approaches, on datasets like Eyeful Tower [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High rendering speed, high fidelity for thin structures and semi-opaque areas, real-time capability.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the surfaceness grid initialization and training phases. Sphere tracing assumes the SDF is well-behaved, which may not hold in all volumetric regions if $\beta$ is too low [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is accelerated by sphere tracing, which requires far fewer samples per ray than uniform volumetric sampling, as the model behaves as a valid SDF in >95% of the scene [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The model uses dense multi-resolution 3D feature grids, multi-resolution triplanes, and a $512^3$ surfaceness grid [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** HybridNeRF improves error rates by 15–30% compared to state-of-the-art baselines, including rasterization-based approaches, while achieving real-time framerates (at least 36 FPS) for virtual-reality resolutions (2K×2K) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High rendering efficiency due to surface-like behavior in most of the scene; high fidelity in challenging regions (thin structures, semi-opaque areas) due to volumetric modeling; real-time performance.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the surfaceness grid and Eikonal loss; may struggle with highly complex view-dependent effects if not properly modeled [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering is required and the scene contains a mix of solid surfaces and thin/semi-opaque structures.
+*   **When not to use:** For scenes that are entirely volumetric (e.g., smoke, fire) or entirely solid without thin structures, pure volumetric or pure surface methods may be more appropriate.
 
 ## Background
-HybridNeRF was introduced by Haithem Turki et al. in 2024 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of pure surface-based neural fields, which struggle with thin structures and view-dependent effects like refraction and translucency, and pure volumetric NeRFs, which are slow to render. The method was developed to enable efficient rendering for immersive applications such as augmented reality and virtual teleconferencing [S1].
+HybridNeRF was proposed by Haithem Turki et al. in 2024 [S1]. It builds on previous work in surface-based neural fields, such as VolSDF [S1], which interprets MLP outputs as SDFs. The key innovation is the introduction of spatially-varying surfaceness parameters $\beta(\mathbf{x})$, replacing the global $\beta$ used in prior work [S1]. The method was evaluated on the Eyeful Tower dataset [S1] and other commonly used view synthesis datasets.
 
